# Manual examples for an HTTP client. Replace placeholders from actual responses.
# Development loopback only; use HTTPS in deployment.
@base = http://127.0.0.1:3000/api
@token = REPLACE_WITH_JWT_FROM_LOGIN
@verificationToken = REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARACTERS

### Health: 200 when connected
GET {{base}}/health

### Register: 201; public role is always CLIENT
POST {{base}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{"name":"QA Client","email":"qa.client@example.com","password":"Stage1-example-password!"}

### Verification: 200 once; replay 400
POST {{base}}/auth/verify-email
Content-Type: application/json

{"token":"{{verificationToken}}"}

### Resend: 202, previous token invalidated
POST {{base}}/auth/resend-verification
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"qa.client@example.com"}

### Login: 200; copy data.accessToken into token above
POST {{base}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"qa.client@example.com","password":"Stage1-example-password!"}

### Own profile: 200 with active token
GET {{base}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Administrator session: CLIENT gets 403; ADMINISTRATOR gets 200
GET {{base}}/admin/session
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Logout current session: 200; subsequent me request gets 401
POST {{base}}/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### Reject public privilege escalation: 400
POST {{base}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{"name":"QA Client","email":"qa.other@example.com","password":"Stage1-example-password!","role":"ADMINISTRATOR"}
